Result: previous fix holds after batching restoration.
Evidence at 100000 msg/s, k=8, 500000 messages:
sender: sent=500000 packets=562848 lapped=0 sendmmsg_calls=362046 send_calls=0 max_sendmmsg_batch=32 fec_parity_sent=62848 fec_closed_by_k=62132 fec_closed_by_timeout=715 fec_closed_by_flush=1 fec_data_bytes=168000128 fec_parity_bytes=37299456 fec_overhead_pct=22.202040 fec_arrival_p50_ns=9896 fec_arrival_p99_ns=61885 fec_arrival_max_ns=10430603 test_dropped=0 test_reordered=0
Expected parity from full k=8 groups is 500000 / 8 = 62500. Observed parity is 62848, close to expected. Timeouts are 715, not the previous 147320-class runaway.

Evidence at 300000 msg/s, k=8, 500000 requested messages:
sender: sent=426612 packets=480128 lapped=7722 sendmmsg_calls=15614 send_calls=0 max_sendmmsg_batch=32 fec_parity_sent=53516 fec_closed_by_k=53131 fec_closed_by_timeout=385 fec_closed_by_flush=0 fec_data_bytes=143268288 fec_parity_bytes=31753560 fec_overhead_pct=22.163705 fec_arrival_p50_ns=382 fec_arrival_p99_ns=75133 fec_arrival_max_ns=11316451 test_dropped=0 test_reordered=0
This run is WSL-saturated and lapped 7722 times, so it is not a valid absolute throughput result. The FEC closure pattern still shows most generations closing by k, not by timeout.

